[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] **Episode:** Days 285–292, City of London cluster. Seven sites measured (A-305–A-311). All eight London PRIOR sites now complete.
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] 
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] **The five PRIOR sites in the square mile** answer the episode's question — what was Tyler doing in the City of London across four centuries: he was living there, using the square mile as a home geography in the pre-documentation period. The PRIOR constructions are the residential footprint.
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] 
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] **The two anchoring constructions:**
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] - **A-306, Aldersgate Street, September 1563** — 71ft, the plague year. Tyler stayed when others fled. His journal: *"The street was almost empty. Two or three left who could not leave. I sat with the air for a morning."* The Camera Op's attention was caught here: *not to what was gone — to what was still here.*
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] - **A-308, St. Paul's Cathedral churchyard, November 15, 1623** — 88ft, AMPLIFICATION. The day after Gray's Inn. *"I did not know what to do with myself. I came here."* Two constructions from the most important week in four hundred years: 188ft from the afternoon, 88ft from the day after. Both now in the record. The Syndicate and the Parliament were both in London that week and documented neither.
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] 
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] **The Camera Op's third option** found its shape at Aldersgate Street: presence to absence — witnessing the space a departed thing left, not the thing itself. The plague-year street. The 1553 crowd around an announcement. Name still pending.
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] 
[2026-06-10 20:24:17]   [TYLER] The Exchange Student spread her map at Cheapside on Day 292 and said: *"I need a bigger map for the City."*
